{
  "result": "The best ideas arrive while you're doing the dishes.\n\nNot when you're staring at a blank screen, trying to be brilliant, but when your hands are busy and your mind is allowed to wander. The water is warm, a plate is half-scrubbed, and suddenly a sentence you've been hunting for weeks just walks into the kitchen.\n\nI think we design our lives backwards. We schedule blocks for 'creative thinking' and 'deep work' as if insight punches a time clock. But the brain is not an employee. It's more like a cat. It comes when you stop calling it.\n\nThat's why I love trains, and long walks without podcasts, and waiting in line at the post office. Those are the unmonetized gaps where your mind finally gets bored enough to make something new. We fill every crack with content now, scrolling, listening, optimizing, and then wonder why we feel so uninspired.\n\nThere's a lighthouse near where I grew up that hasn't worked in thirty years. The coast guard left it, but the caretaker still paints it every spring. White paint over white paint, for no ships. I asked him once why he bothers. He said, \"Someone might still be looking.\"\n\nMaybe that's what writing is. Or kindness. Or remembering to look up at the moon.\n\nYou keep doing the work even when the light is technically out. And maybe, if you keep it clean enough, someone else will find their way home too.",
